Example reward: {'score': 3.5, 'response': 'To solve this problem efficiently, we\'ll go through every substring and compute its beauty as the difference between the frequency of the most frequent and least frequent character. However, directly generating and processing all substrings can be computationally expensive for large strings.\n\nBut since the string length is at most 500, and we\'re allowed to use that limit, we can afford a brute-force method that iterates over all substrings and counts frequency of characters within each substring.\n\nHere is the implementation that computes the sum of the beauty of all substrings using brute force, with the described class and method structure:\n\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def beautySum(self, s: str) -> int:\n        total_beauty = 0\n        n = len(s)\n        for i in range(n):\n            freq = {}\n            max_freq = 0\n            min_freq = 1000\n            for j in range(i, n):\n                char = s[j]\n                freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n                max_freq = max(max_freq, freq[char])\n                min_freq = min(min_freq, freq[char])\n                # if the current substring length is at least 2, calculate its beauty\n                if j - i + 1 > 1:\n                    total_beauty += (max_freq - min_freq)\n        return total_beauty\n\n    def run_tests(self):\n        print(self.beautySum("aabcb"))\n        print(self.beautySum("aabcbaa"))\n\n# Run the tests\nsol = Solution()\nsol.run_tests()\n```\n\nThis implementation iterates over all substrings and computes the beauty using the frequency counts within the substring. The `beautySum` method is responsible for the core logic, and `run_tests` helps demonstrate the function by calling the method on two example inputs.\n\nThe key here is to iterate efficiently and compute frequency counts without overcounting. It handles the constraints well within the given limit of up to 500 characters.\n\nThis should provide cor